    {
      "n": 3,
      "title": "Proteins",
      "competency": "STEM_BIO11/12-II-i-j-15",
      "content": "<h3>🎯 Learning Objectives</h3><ul><li>Describe the structure of proteins.</li><li>Explain the four levels of protein structure.</li><li>List the functions of proteins.</li><li>Relate protein structure to function.</li></ul><h3>🔑 Key Concepts</h3><ul><li><strong>Amino acid</strong> — the monomer of proteins.</li><li><strong>Peptide bond</strong> — bond between amino acids.</li><li><strong>Denaturation</strong> — loss of protein shape/function.</li><li><strong>Primary, secondary, tertiary, quaternary</strong> — four levels of protein structure.</li></ul><h3>📚 Lesson Content</h3><h4>A. Amino Acids</h4><p>20 different amino acids. Each has an amino group (–NH₂), a carboxyl group (–COOH), and a variable R group.</p><h4>B. Levels of Protein Structure</h4><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Primary</td><td>Sequence of amino acids</td></tr><tr><td>Secondary</td><td>Alpha helix or beta sheet (H-bonds)</td></tr><tr><td>Tertiary</td><td>3D folding of the whole chain</td></tr><tr><td>Quaternary</td><td>Multiple chains (e.g., hemoglobin)</td></tr></tbody></table><h4>C. Functions of Proteins</h4><ul><li>Enzymes (catalysts)</li><li>Structural (collagen, keratin)</li><li>Transport (hemoglobin)</li><li>Signaling (hormones like insulin)</li><li>Defense (antibodies)</li><li>Movement (actin, myosin)</li></ul>",
      "activity1": {
        "timeLimit": 90,
        "badgeId": "protein-master",
        "badgeName": "Protein Master",
        "badgeIcon": "🥚",
        "pairs": [
          { "key": "aa", "left": "Amino acid", "right": "Monomer of proteins" },
          { "key": "pep", "left": "Peptide bond", "right": "Connects amino acids" },
          { "key": "prim", "left": "Primary structure", "right": "Amino acid sequence" },
          { "key": "sec", "left": "Secondary", "right": "Alpha helix or beta sheet" },
